[size=5][color=#D0B17A][b]Description[/b][/color][/size]

How should a slim, muscular or rounded human look after becoming a werewolf?

CLAW's Morphbench plugin lets you work on that connection with both bodies in view. Change the human's body morphs and see the corresponding wolf beside them. Turn either model to turn the other, then compare their proportions from the same angle.

The human sliders are your inputs. The wolf's sliders show the result of the selected conversion. You can tune that conversion, compare it with the original and save your own wolf preset.

[size=5][color=#D0B17A][b]What you can do[/b][/color][/size]

[list]
[*]Compare male or female human and werewolf bodies side by side.
[*]Import BodySlide presets or effective human morph values, then adjust the human sliders.
[*]Tune how much each human morph contributes to each wolf morph, including nonlinear relationships.
[*]Save and share conversion presets, or create a fixed wolf shape that stays independent of the human body.
[*]Inspect the resulting values and vertex displacement, and export them for further analysis.
[/list]

This is an authoring plugin for Morphbench. It works outside the game. Applying changes updates the two previews; it does not install changes into Skyrim or assign bodies to actors.

[size=5][color=#D0B17A][b]Requirements[/b][/color][/size]

[list]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/194795]Morphbench Application[/url] 0.8.5 or later, with its required Morphbench Launcher v1. [url=https://github.com/Vhodnoylogin/morphbench/releases/tag/v0.8.5]Matching Application and Launcher downloads on GitHub[/url] are available if a Nexus file is unavailable.
[*]Your own human body NIF and TRI resources: [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/46311]HIMBO[/url] 5.8 for male comparisons, or [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/198]CBBE[/url] 2.0.3 / [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/30174]CBBE 3BA[/url] 2.48 for female comparisons. Choose the family you intend to compare; 3BA is an optional CBBE extension.
[*][url=https://github.com/Vhodnoylogin/claw/releases/tag/claw/0.7.0.0]CLAW 0.7.0.0 werewolf resources[/url]. Use the NIF/TRI files from the working archive; the separate test_CLAW archive is unnecessary. The older 0.6.2.0 bodies lack the Lean morphs used by this plugin's default matrices.
[/list]

Human bodies, werewolf bodies and textures are not included in this download. OBody and RaceMenu are not needed to use the offline comparison itself. They remain part of the separate in-game setup when applicable.

[size=5][color=#D0B17A][b]Getting started[/b][/color][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Open Morphbench's plugin manager, install this ZIP and enable it. Keep the ZIP intact for the manager; do not install it as a Skyrim Data mod.
[*]Open the CLAW human/wolf comparison tab and select your human resource family and matching human/wolf models from Morphbench's catalogue.
[*]Click [b]Open comparison pair[/b]. Import a human preset or adjust the human sliders.
[*]Click [b]Calculate / Apply to both previews[/b] to inspect the result. You can optionally enable live application for subsequent slider changes.
[*]Open [b]Wolf preset — coefficient and function matrix[/b] to tune the conversion. Export the wolf preset to keep your work, or export the mapping XML for the CLAW runtime workflow.
[/list]

When updating an existing installation, disable the plugin, use the manager's update operation with the replacement ZIP, then enable it again. Existing CLAW development installations use the same plugin ID.

[size=5][color=#D0B17A][b]Current scope[/b][/color][/size]

The supplied conversion covers body proportions. Human-to-wolf head, ear, tail and claw relationships, fur colour and physics are separate development work.

The previews show the selected body files in their rest geometry. They help compare shape and measurements; textured appearance, animation and in-game behaviour still need their own checks. For an endpoint comparison, select the corresponding _0 or _1 models in both panels.

[size=5][color=#D0B17A][b]Technical guide for modders[/b][/color][/size]
[spoiler]
A wolf preset is an ordered sparse coefficient-and-function matrix. Several human morphs can contribute to one wolf morph, and one human morph can affect several wolf morphs. Constant rules can define an independent wolf shape.

The mathematical evaluator is the same portable C++ source compiled into the CLAW game DLL and into WebAssembly for this plugin. The plugin does not duplicate the conversion formula in JavaScript.

Supported functions are linear, positive, negative, smoothstep, integer power and constant. The editor reports each contribution, the final target value and differences from the original matrix or an imported coefficient snapshot. Wolf output values remain read-only; edit the human inputs or the conversion rules to change them.

Preset JSON preserves family, sex, calibration, ordered rules, coefficients, caps and function parameters. Exported mapping XML preserves the complete native family catalogue and explicit defaults. Exporting a file does not install or apply that file to a running game. Existing saved actors retain their saved matrices until explicitly rebound through CLAW's runtime API.

Vertex inspection reads each preview's complete current and initial positions, calculates current-minus-initial displacement and exports the result as JSON. These are sequential, model-space reads from fixed NIF resources, not measurements of animated game skinning. The plugin rejects a retained snapshot if the input morph vectors change during collection.

BodySlide small/big coefficients can be interpolated for a chosen weight, but the selected fixed NIF is not automatically interpolated between its neutral _0 and _1 geometry. Effective human JSON is already effective and is not multiplied by weight again.

Large geometry exports may exceed the host's default download limit. Configure Morphbench's prepared-export limit when needed; the host supports up to 32 MiB. The pageable text viewer retains the complete export rather than only its displayed page.

